WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.272
Begin with the complete memory budget.

00:00:02.272 --> 00:00:06.617
The quantized weights must fit alongside runtime buffers and the key-value cache.

00:00:06.617 --> 00:00:10.600
The cache grows with context length, active sequences, and model architecture.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:12.410
Batch settings add temporary pressure.

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:17.117
Leave headroom instead of selecting a file whose size nearly equals available VRAM.

00:00:17.117 --> 00:00:21.100
Full GPU offload is often fastest when supported and memory allows.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:26.531
Partial offload places some layers on the GPU and leaves the rest on the CPU,

00:00:26.531 --> 00:00:30.876
which can enable a larger model but increase transfer and CPU work.

00:00:30.876 --> 00:00:36.669
CPU-only inference remains practical for smaller quantized models when system RAM and memory bandwidth are sufficient.

00:00:36.669 --> 00:00:39.566
Unified-memory systems share capacity between CPU and GPU,

00:00:39.566 --> 00:00:43.548
so the model competes with the operating system and other applications.

00:00:43.548 --> 00:00:47.169
Context can change a successful startup into an allocation failure.

00:00:47.169 --> 00:00:50.790
Test the configured maximum as well as a normal prompt.

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:54.772
Measure time to first token, prompt-processing speed, generation speed, VRAM, RAM,

00:00:54.772 --> 00:00:55.497
and stability.

00:00:55.497 --> 00:00:58.393
A model that barely fits may run slowly,

00:00:58.393 --> 00:01:03.462
prevent other applications from using the GPU, or fail when a long document arrives.

00:01:03.462 --> 00:01:05.634
Optimize for the actual service envelope.

00:01:05.634 --> 00:01:09.979
A smaller or slightly more compressed model that remains fully accelerated with

00:01:09.979 --> 00:01:14.324
context headroom may outperform a higher-precision version that spills into system memory.

00:01:14.324 --> 00:01:16.134
Conversely, if both fit comfortably,

00:01:16.134 --> 00:01:19.755
higher precision may offer better quality with little operational cost.

00:01:19.755 --> 00:01:22.290
Hardware fit determines which comparison is meaningful;

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:24.100
measurements determine which option wins.
